{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-8fe291f9-e50c-4180-b91a-7bbedb6732e5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b6fff17f3c0bb1a3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-vy4cxq6w/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-decomposition.md","content":"# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n\nStrategy RHAISTRAT-8009 makes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server, and extends the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute (split-server topology) with artifact traffic routed from the main tracking deployment. All work is owned by the MLflow Team across three active components: MLflow, MLflow Operator, and mlflow-kubernetes-plugins.\n\nThe strategy contains two genuine unknowns that change what downstream epics do, both explicitly flagged as open questions: (1) whether workspace resolution in `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` is coupled to tracking store initialization, and (2) how artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated instance should be configured — with two bounded outcomes (gateway-level routing vs tracking-server delegation). Each becomes an Investigation epic; the routing investigation produces conditional decomposition branches.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Component (canonical) |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|----------------------|\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigation: Workspace resolution initialization coupling in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 | MLflow (spans mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigation: Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 | MLflow (spans mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | MLflow server workspace-aware artifacts-only serving mode | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 | MLflow |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow operator dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Cross-component integration testing for workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans all three strategy components) |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E007 | Gateway-level path routing for artifact traffic (branch A) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking server artifact delegation configuration (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow |\n| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E008 | Operator wiring for tracking server artifact delegation (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n\nMain-plan epics: E001–E006 (6). Branch epics: 3 (conditional alternatives under the routing investigation, not standalone Jira issues). Total: 9.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[\"E001 INV: plugin coupling (P0)\"] --> E003[\"E003 Plugin resolution (P0)\"]\n    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Server artifacts-only mode (P0)\"]\n    E002[\"E002 INV: routing design (P1)\"] --> E005[\"E005 Operator dedicated instance (P1)\"]\n    E003 --> E004\n    E003 --> E006[\"E006 Integration testing (P1)\"]\n    E004 --> E006\n    E005 --> E006\n    E002 -.-> BEA[\"BRANCH-A-E007 Gateway path routing (P1)\"]\n    E002 -.-> BEB7[\"BRANCH-B-E007 Server delegation config (P1)\"]\n    E002 -.-> BEB8[\"BRANCH-B-E008 Operator delegation wiring (P1)\"]\n    BEB7 --> BEB8\n```\n\nSolid arrows are main-plan edges (dependency must complete before dependent starts). Dashed arrows gate conditional branch epics; branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n\nMain-plan critical path: E001 → E003 → E004 → E006 (length 4).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The coupling answer determines whether the plugin work is verification-only or a refactoring of workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — a scope rewrite, hence a true gate (`gated_by`, action `rewrite`). |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The server's workspace-provider-only initialization approach depends on the same outcome; approach rewrites if the provider is coupled to tracking stores. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (library → consumer) | The server's per-request workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution capability in the artifacts-only context; the server epic's own ACs (HTTP 403 on non-membership, fail-closed 503) cannot pass without functioning resolution. |\n| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The routing outcome determines the dedicated instance's HTTPRoute/exposure design (external path-routed vs internal behind delegation) — scope rewrite. |\n| E003 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 | Rule 13 / strategy integration-testing scope | The integration suite builds against all three implementation deliverables; the strategy allocates integration testing (multi-tenant scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed, split-server routing) as its own 1–2 sprint work unit. |\n| E002 → BRANCH-A-E007, BRANCH-B-E007, BRANCH-B-E008 | Rule 3 + conditional branches | Bounded outcomes (≤3): Outcome A (gateway-level routing suffices) vs Outcome B (tracking-server delegation required). Each branch implements HLR-6 per its outcome. |\n| BRANCH-B-E007 → BRANCH-B-E008 | Rule 13 (artifact → consumer) | The operator wiring renders the delegation configuration contract produced by the server-side delegation work; sibling reference within the same branch. |\n\nDeliberate non-edges:\n\n- **E005 ∥ E003/E004 (parallel):** the operator renders configuration (flag combinations, workspace settings) for the server — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact (Rule 13 exception, same philosophy as image digests). The coupling is expressed as an AC gate on E005 (the rendered artifacts-only command must run the workspace-aware flag combination against a server image that supports it) and validated end-to-end in E006, never as a DAG edge.\n- **E001 ∥ E002 (parallel):** independent unknowns resolved by different experiments with different deliverables — not combined.\n- **No onboarding chain (Rule 20/21 N/A):** all three components exist in the platform — architecture context files exist (mlflow.md, mlflow-operator.md) and mlflow-kubernetes-plugins is documented in mlflow.md as the packaged `kubernetes-auth` plugin (v1.5.0).\n- **No docs-authoring epic:** Documentation Support is pending review (not \"Yes\") and the strategy contains no substantive content authoring; incidental doc touch-ups fold into the originating epics' scope.\n- **No external dependency with uncertain timing:** all four strategy dependencies (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy) are internal and exist — no tiered delivery split.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | E004 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; denial = HTTP 403 | E004 (primary), E003 (enabling capability), E006 (end-to-end verification) |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | E004, E006 |\n| HLR-4 | P0 | Single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | E004, E006 |\n| HLR-5 | P1 | Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | E005, E006 |\n| HLR-6 | P1 | Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | BRANCH-A-E007 (outcome A) or BRANCH-B-E007 + BRANCH-B-E008 (outcome B), E006 |\n| HLR-7 | P1 | Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | E003, E006 |\n| HLR-8 | P2 | Audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | E004 (bundled) |\n\nBundling decisions recorded in the matrix:\n\n- **HLR-8 (P2) bundled into E004 (P0):** audit logging of workspace denials is emitted from the exact denial code path HLR-2's P0 validation work builds — the same request-handling surface and call sites. Splitting it out would force a second pass over the same artifact-request denial path for a few log lines; it is not separately schedulable and ships no independent user value beyond the denial behavior itself. HLR-8 also already pins the channel to server logs (see Health Warnings for the open telemetry-channel question).\n- **HLR-2 spans E003 and E004 by architecture, not by duplication:** the server (E004) implements request validation; the plugin (E003) provides the workspace resolution that validation delegates to. They are separate components (Rule 1) in separate repos producing complementary halves of one capability — not two epics building the same surface.\n\n## Conditional Decomposition Branches\n\nThe routing design investigation (E002) has two bounded outcomes that change which downstream epics exist:\n\n- **Outcome A — gateway-level routing suffices:** artifact API traffic is routed to the dedicated instance via Gateway API HTTPRoute path rules; no tracking server change. Branch epic: `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E007` (Operator).\n- **Outcome B — tracking-server delegation required:** the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. Branch epics: `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007` (MLflow server: delegation configuration) and `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E008` (Operator: wiring the delegation configuration into the main tracking Deployment — Rule 1 component boundary keeps the server-side and operator-side halves in separate epics).\n\nBoth branches cover HLR-6; exactly one is implemented. Branch epics are gated by E002 (`gated_by`, `branch`, `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — if the other outcome holds, the epic does not exist). They are attached to the investigation as a plan document, not created as standalone Jira issues, and no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\nPer Step 1.5, Staff Engineer / SME Input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach. Three overrides applied:\n\n1. **No new flag.** The originally proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed everywhere. E004's scope explicitly requires that no new server flag be introduced — workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`, and an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` is required when both are set.\n2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** E005 is scoped as a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute plus main-deployment artifact traffic routing — not a single CR field with Helm values propagation.\n3. **SSAR detail over-specified.** No epic prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview or other Kubernetes API authorization mechanics; epic scopes state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure, and the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup is unchanged.\n\nThe strategy body already incorporates these corrections (the Technical Approach cites them); they are logged here for traceability and enforced in the epic acceptance criteria.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\nThe canonical RHAI component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains `MLflow` but has no separate entries for `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. All epics therefore use the canonical name `MLflow`, and each epic's span is noted in the Epic List and in the epic files. `submit.py` silently strips non-canonical component names, so no epic uses \"MLflow Operator\" or \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" as its `component` value.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Pending strategy-level process gates.** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. These are strategy-level gates, not per-epic implementation gates, so they are recorded here rather than as epic ACs; implementation start may be gated on their completion.\n- **Audit telemetry channel open (strategy Open Question, owner PM/Security).** HLR-8 pins audit logging to server logs, so E004 implements that and the question does not change epic structure (Rule 4: informational for the P0/P1 structure). If PM/Security later selects a different channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log), that is follow-up scope on top of E004's denial-path logging.\n- **Latency threshold undefined (strategy Open Question, owner PM/Engineering).** No latency target is specified. E004 measures and documents per-request validation overhead; short-TTL caching (consistent with existing auth-path caching patterns) is deferred until a threshold is set. Rework risk if the threshold demands caching at launch.\n- **E006 priority is P1 although it verifies P0 behaviors.** The P0 HLRs (HLR-1..4) are implemented and verified within E004's own acceptance criteria (the strategy's ACs 1–4 are server-level). E006's distinct scope is the assembled split-server topology (P1 HLRs 5–6) plus holistic regression; scheduling it at P1 keeps the P0 delivery chain free of P1-gated operator work.\n- **E005 kept above the ~2-week guideline.** Estimated 2–3 sprints. It is one logical change — the split-server topology rendered through the operator's Helm-in-operator pattern — and splitting Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templating, flag propagation, and config validation across epics would fragment one chart-extension effort and duplicate template scaffolding. Re-split trigger: if adding the second Deployment requires structural Helm chart rework beyond adding templates (the strategy's recorded assumption), split the chart restructuring from the instance templating at that point.\n- **Artifact path resolution scope boundary.** Path-to-experiment resolution is scoped to the S3 artifact store's standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` layout (the standard RHOAI configuration, per the strategy's risk mitigation); limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented in E004 rather than solved.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. All strategy dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The parallelism that matters here is structural: the server/plugin chain (E001 → E003 → E004) and the operator chain (E002 → E005) proceed independently and converge in E006.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":70330,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94604f82b408a2f8","agent_id":"a4d4447c1339d2689","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-23c6bb583974","uuid":"587f3c9c-8f8b-4f93-8088-4075e14fd8e2","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:40:28.614Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
