{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-b748beae-c081-4452-bf03-887775408d9e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b05a66a6b08843d5","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-vy4cxq6w/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E002.md","content":"# Investigation: Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nDecide how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance: whether gateway-level routing (Gateway API HTTPRoute path rules) suffices with no tracking server change, or whether the tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. The answer determines the HTTPRoute design for both deployments and whether server-side delegation work exists.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze how artifact API traffic reaches the artifacts-only proxy today, and which routing surfaces (Gateway API HTTPRoute path/host rules, created under the operator's existing runtime CRD discovery pattern for optional dependencies) are available.\n- Determine whether path-based gateway routing can direct artifact API traffic from the main tracking host to the dedicated artifacts-only instance with no tracking server configuration change.\n- If gateway-level routing is insufficient, determine what tracking-server configuration would be required to delegate artifact operations to a dedicated instance (configuration surface and accepted flags/environment).\n- Assess the implications for the dedicated instance's HTTPRoute and exposure design (externally path-routed vs. internal behind delegation).\n- Classify the outcome as Outcome A (gateway-level routing suffices, no server change) or Outcome B (tracking-server delegation configuration required), and hand the selected branch its design.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A decision is recorded classifying the outcome as A or B, backed by evidence from the operator and server codebases and the Gateway API routing capabilities in use.\n- If Outcome A: the path/host matching design is documented, including which artifact request paths must route to the dedicated instance and which traffic must remain on the main tracking server.\n- If Outcome B: the required tracking-server configuration surface is identified and documented precisely enough to implement the delegation.\n- The HTTPRoute/exposure design implications for the dedicated artifacts-only instance are documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Enables HLR-6 (P1 — main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance) and informs the HTTPRoute design portion of HLR-5 (P1 — dedicated Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute). No HLR is implemented directly by this investigation.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":85201,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94604f82b408a2f8","agent_id":"a4d4447c1339d2689","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-23c6bb583974","uuid":"1ea1ba28-80ac-409e-a745-6003dd66c516","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:40:46.107Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
