{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-3cd03f63-8819-47d7-b92b-dff27efacd78","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f30ab23b23a1f76","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-vy4cxq6w/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E004.md","content":"# MLflow server workspace-aware artifacts-only serving mode\n\n## Description\n\nMake `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set with an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, the server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, validates every artifact request against workspace membership (denying non-members with HTTP 403), and fails closed when the workspace store is unreachable. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer input).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` in that combination — the server fails to start with a clear error if it is missing.\n- Initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- For each artifact request: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure — no new authorization mechanism and no new server flag.\n- Fail-closed behavior: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable), never served without validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without workspace flags retains current behavior; omitting either flag preserves current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (bundled here because it is emitted from the same denial code path the validation work builds; a different telemetry channel remains an open PM/Security question and would be follow-up scope).\n- Measure the per-request workspace validation latency overhead and document the results; short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions (consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path) is deferred until the PM/Engineering latency threshold is set.\n- Scope artifact path resolution to the S3 artifact store's standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure (the standard RHOAI configuration); document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The server starts with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and initializes the workspace provider while tracking and model registry stores remain uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- The server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but without `--workspace-store-uri` fails to start with a clear error.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- With the workspace store unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable — no artifact is served without validation.\n- With `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, artifacts are served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved).\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit log entries in the server logs.\n- The per-request workspace validation latency overhead is measured and documented.\n- No new server CLI flag is introduced, and no existing flag, API contract, or configuration key changes meaning.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` — implemented here.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership with HTTP 403 denials — implemented here.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable — implemented here.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant and no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior — implemented here.\n- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs — emitted from the same denial code path as HLR-2; not separately schedulable without a second pass over the same request-handling surface, so it is bundled at the higher epic priority.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":86716,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94604f82b408a2f8","agent_id":"a4d4447c1339d2689","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-23c6bb583974","uuid":"344155fb-92cd-4e20-9808-7d81fe836be4","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:41:04.146Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
