{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-89ed920d-b2c4-4fdb-a09c-a92ac6b50f4c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7429c0edab17775","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-vy4cxq6w/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E005.md","content":"# MLflow operator dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment\n\n## Description\n\nExtend the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute — the split-server topology — and propagate workspace configuration to it using the same mechanisms as the main tracking deployment. Users continue to configure a single MLflow CR; the operator manages the split topology declaratively.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New Helm chart templates within the operator's existing Helm-in-operator pattern (MLflow CR spec converted to Helm values, rendered through embedded chart templates, applied via server-side apply) for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, separate from the main tracking Deployment.\n- Propagate workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms used for the main tracking deployment; the artifacts-only container command runs `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`.\n- Create HTTPRoute resources conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery, consistent with the operator's existing pattern for optional dependencies; the instance's route/exposure design follows the routing design investigation's outcome.\n- Deploy the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar on the artifacts-only instance identically to the main tracking deployment — the authentication layer is unchanged.\n- Operator validation: reject MLflow CR configurations where split-server artifact serving is enabled but the artifacts-only instance would lack workspace flags, since misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation.\n- Early in the epic, validate the recorded assumption that the Helm chart rendering supports adding a second Deployment without structural chart changes; if structural rework is needed, complete it within this epic before templating the instance.\n- Configuration of artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment is not in this epic's scope: it is implemented per the routing design decision (gateway-level path routing, or tracking-server delegation plus its operator wiring).\n- Coupling to the server capability is an acceptance-criterion gate, not a scheduling dependency: the rendered artifacts-only command must run against an MLflow server image that supports the workspace-aware artifacts-only flag combination.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Reconciling an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute distinct from the main tracking Deployment, measured by the presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations.\n- The rendered artifacts-only Deployment container command runs `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts`, verified by inspecting the rendered Deployment spec.\n- Workspace configuration is propagated to the artifacts-only instance through the same mechanisms as the main tracking deployment.\n- HTTPRoute creation follows the existing conditional runtime CRD discovery pattern (no hard Gateway API dependency when the CRDs are absent).\n- The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar is deployed on the artifacts-only instance, configured identically to the main tracking deployment.\n- The operator rejects CR configurations where the artifacts-only instance would lack workspace flags, with a clear validation error.\n- Deployed against an MLflow server image supporting the workspace-aware artifacts-only flag combination, the dedicated instance serves workspace-validated artifacts.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-5 (P1): operator deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute — implemented here.\n- Supports HLR-6 (P1): the routing configuration over this instance is implemented per the routing design decision and validated with the assembled topology.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":88520,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-94604f82b408a2f8","agent_id":"a4d4447c1339d2689","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-23c6bb583974","uuid":"16518340-b5f8-496d-a16f-d1c61d734ee0","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:41:14.698Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009 into epics"}
