{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af7df17400c8ad89","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 9\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8009 — Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n11\t\n12\tStrategy RHAISTRAT-8009 makes `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server, and extends the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute (split-server topology) with artifact traffic routed from the main tracking deployment. All work is owned by the MLflow Team across three active components: MLflow, MLflow Operator, and mlflow-kubernetes-plugins.\n13\t\n14\tThe strategy contains two genuine unknowns that change what downstream epics do, both explicitly flagged as open questions: (1) whether workspace resolution in `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` is coupled to tracking store initialization, and (2) how artifact traffic routing from the main tracking server to the dedicated instance should be configured — with two bounded outcomes (gateway-level routing vs tracking-server delegation). Each becomes an Investigation epic; the routing investigation produces conditional decomposition branches.\n15\t\n16\t## Epic List\n17\t\n18\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority | Component (canonical) |\n19\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|----------------------|\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigation: Workspace resolution initialization coupling in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 | MLflow (spans mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigation: Artifact traffic routing design for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 | MLflow (spans mlflow-kubernetes-plugins) |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | MLflow server workspace-aware artifacts-only serving mode | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 | MLflow |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | MLflow operator dedicated artifacts-only instance deployment | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | Cross-component integration testing for workspace-aware artifact serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans all three strategy components) |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E007 | Gateway-level path routing for artifact traffic (branch A) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking server artifact delegation configuration (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E008 | Operator wiring for tracking server artifact delegation (branch B) | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 | MLflow (spans MLflow Operator) |\n29\t\n30\tMain-plan epics: E001–E006 (6). Branch epics: 3 (conditional alternatives under the routing investigation, not standalone Jira issues). Total: 9.\n31\t\n32\t## Dependency DAG\n33\t\n34\t```mermaid\n35\tgraph TD\n36\t    E001[\"E001 INV: plugin coupling (P0)\"] --> E003[\"E003 Plugin resolution (P0)\"]\n37\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 Server artifacts-only mode (P0)\"]\n38\t    E002[\"E002 INV: routing design (P1)\"] --> E005[\"E005 Operator dedicated instance (P1)\"]\n39\t    E003 --> E004\n40\t    E003 --> E006[\"E006 Integration testing (P1)\"]\n41\t    E004 --> E006\n42\t    E005 --> E006\n43\t    E002 -.-> BEA[\"BRANCH-A-E007 Gateway path routing (P1)\"]\n44\t    E002 -.-> BEB7[\"BRANCH-B-E007 Server delegation config (P1)\"]\n45\t    E002 -.-> BEB8[\"BRANCH-B-E008 Operator delegation wiring (P1)\"]\n46\t    BEB7 --> BEB8\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\tSolid arrows are main-plan edges (dependency must complete before dependent starts). Dashed arrows gate conditional branch epics; branch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path.\n50\t\n51\tMain-plan critical path: E001 → E003 → E004 → E006 (length 4).\n52\t\n53\t## DAG Justification\n54\t\n55\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n56\t|------|------|-----------|\n57\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The coupling answer determines whether the plugin work is verification-only or a refactoring of workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — a scope rewrite, hence a true gate (`gated_by`, action `rewrite`). |\n58\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The server's workspace-provider-only initialization approach depends on the same outcome; approach rewrites if the provider is coupled to tracking stores. |\n59\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (library → consumer) | The server's per-request workspace validation builds against the plugin's workspace resolution capability in the artifacts-only context; the server epic's own ACs (HTTP 403 on non-membership, fail-closed 503) cannot pass without functioning resolution. |\n60\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (investigation gate) | The routing outcome determines the dedicated instance's HTTPRoute/exposure design (external path-routed vs internal behind delegation) — scope rewrite. |\n61\t| E003 → E006, E004 → E006, E005 → E006 | Rule 13 / strategy integration-testing scope | The integration suite builds against all three implementation deliverables; the strategy allocates integration testing (multi-tenant scenarios, backwards compatibility, single-tenant regression, fail-closed, split-server routing) as its own 1–2 sprint work unit. |\n62\t| E002 → BRANCH-A-E007, BRANCH-B-E007, BRANCH-B-E008 | Rule 3 + conditional branches | Bounded outcomes (≤3): Outcome A (gateway-level routing suffices) vs Outcome B (tracking-server delegation required). Each branch implements HLR-6 per its outcome. |\n63\t| BRANCH-B-E007 → BRANCH-B-E008 | Rule 13 (artifact → consumer) | The operator wiring renders the delegation configuration contract produced by the server-side delegation work; sibling reference within the same branch. |\n64\t\n65\tDeliberate non-edges:\n66\t\n67\t- **E005 ∥ E003/E004 (parallel):** the operator renders configuration (flag combinations, workspace settings) for the server — a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact (Rule 13 exception, same philosophy as image digests). The coupling is expressed as an AC gate on E005 (the rendered artifacts-only command must run the workspace-aware flag combination against a server image that supports it) and validated end-to-end in E006, never as a DAG edge.\n68\t- **E001 ∥ E002 (parallel):** independent unknowns resolved by different experiments with different deliverables — not combined.\n69\t- **No onboarding chain (Rule 20/21 N/A):** all three components exist in the platform — architecture context files exist (mlflow.md, mlflow-operator.md) and mlflow-kubernetes-plugins is documented in mlflow.md as the packaged `kubernetes-auth` plugin (v1.5.0).\n70\t- **No docs-authoring epic:** Documentation Support is pending review (not \"Yes\") and the strategy contains no substantive content authoring; incidental doc touch-ups fold into the originating epics' scope.\n71\t- **No external dependency with uncertain timing:** all four strategy dependencies (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy) are internal and exist — no tiered delivery split.\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n74\t\n75\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n76\t|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|\n77\t| HLR-1 | P0 | `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | E004 |\n78\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; denial = HTTP 403 | E004 (primary), E003 (enabling capability), E006 (end-to-end verification) |\n79\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | E004, E006 |\n80\t| HLR-4 | P0 | Single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | E004, E006 |\n81\t| HLR-5 | P1 | Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | E005, E006 |\n82\t| HLR-6 | P1 | Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | BRANCH-A-E007 (outcome A) or BRANCH-B-E007 + BRANCH-B-E008 (outcome B), E006 |\n83\t| HLR-7 | P1 | Auth plugin workspace resolution works in artifacts-only context without tracking store init | E003, E006 |\n84\t| HLR-8 | P2 | Audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | E004 (bundled) |\n85\t\n86\tBundling decisions recorded in the matrix:\n87\t\n88\t- **HLR-8 (P2) bundled into E004 (P0):** audit logging of workspace denials is emitted from the exact denial code path HLR-2's P0 validation work builds — the same request-handling surface and call sites. Splitting it out would force a second pass over the same artifact-request denial path for a few log lines; it is not separately schedulable and ships no independent user value beyond the denial behavior itself. HLR-8 also already pins the channel to server logs (see Health Warnings for the open telemetry-channel question).\n89\t- **HLR-2 spans E003 and E004 by architecture, not by duplication:** the server (E004) implements request validation; the plugin (E003) provides the workspace resolution that validation delegates to. They are separate components (Rule 1) in separate repos producing complementary halves of one capability — not two epics building the same surface.\n90\t\n91\t## Conditional Decomposition Branches\n92\t\n93\tThe routing design investigation (E002) has two bounded outcomes that change which downstream epics exist:\n94\t\n95\t- **Outcome A — gateway-level routing suffices:** artifact API traffic is routed to the dedicated instance via Gateway API HTTPRoute path rules; no tracking server change. Branch epic: `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-A-E007` (Operator).\n96\t- **Outcome B — tracking-server delegation required:** the tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations. Branch epics: `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007` (MLflow server: delegation configuration) and `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E008` (Operator: wiring the delegation configuration into the main tracking Deployment — Rule 1 component boundary keeps the server-side and operator-side halves in separate epics).\n97\t\n98\tBoth branches cover HLR-6; exactly one is implemented. Branch epics are gated by E002 (`gated_by`, `branch`, `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` — if the other outcome holds, the epic does not exist). They are attached to the investigation as a plan document, not created as standalone Jira issues, and no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its dependencies.\n99\t\n100\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n101\t\n102\tPer Step 1.5, Staff Engineer / SME Input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach. Three overrides applied:\n103\t\n104\t1. **No new flag.** The originally proposed `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field are removed everywhere. E004's scope explicitly requires that no new server flag be introduced — workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`, and an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` is required when both are set.\n105\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle.** E005 is scoped as a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute plus main-deployment artifact traffic routing — not a single CR field with Helm values propagation.\n106\t3. **SSAR detail over-specified.** No epic prescribes SelfSubjectAccessReview or other Kubernetes API authorization mechanics; epic scopes state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure, and the kube-rbac-proxy sidecar / `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup is unchanged.\n107\t\n108\tThe strategy body already incorporates these corrections (the Technical Approach cites them); they are logged here for traceability and enforced in the epic acceptance criteria.\n109\t\n110\t## Component Mapping Notes\n111\t\n112\tThe canonical RHAI component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains `MLflow` but has no separate entries for `MLflow Operator` or `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins`. All epics therefore use the canonical name `MLflow`, and each epic's span is noted in the Epic List and in the epic files. `submit.py` silently strips non-canonical component names, so no epic uses \"MLflow Operator\" or \"mlflow-kubernetes-plugins\" as its `component` value.\n113\t\n114\t## Health Warnings\n115\t\n116\t- **Pending strategy-level process gates.** Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, Accelerator/Package Support, Documentation Support, UXD Support, and Performance Team Support are all \"Pending human review\" in the strategy. These are strategy-level gates, not per-epic implementation gates, so they are recorded here rather than as epic ACs; implementation start may be gated on their completion.\n117\t- **Audit telemetry channel open (strategy Open Question, owner PM/Security).** HLR-8 pins audit logging to server logs, so E004 implements that and the question does not change epic structure (Rule 4: informational for the P0/P1 structure). If PM/Security later selects a different channel (OTLP, Kubernetes audit log), that is follow-up scope on top of E004's denial-path logging.\n118\t- **Latency threshold undefined (strategy Open Question, owner PM/Engineering).** No latency target is specified. E004 measures and documents per-request validation overhead; short-TTL caching (consistent with existing auth-path caching patterns) is deferred until a threshold is set. Rework risk if the threshold demands caching at launch.\n119\t- **E006 priority is P1 although it verifies P0 behaviors.** The P0 HLRs (HLR-1..4) are implemented and verified within E004's own acceptance criteria (the strategy's ACs 1–4 are server-level). E006's distinct scope is the assembled split-server topology (P1 HLRs 5–6) plus holistic regression; scheduling it at P1 keeps the P0 delivery chain free of P1-gated operator work.\n120\t- **E005 kept above the ~2-week guideline.** Estimated 2–3 sprints. It is one logical change — the split-server topology rendered through the operator's Helm-in-operator pattern — and splitting Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templating, flag propagation, and config validation across epics would fragment one chart-extension effort and duplicate template scaffolding. Re-split trigger: if adding the second Deployment requires structural Helm chart rework beyond adding templates (the strategy's recorded assumption), split the chart restructuring from the instance templating at that point.\n121\t- **Artifact path resolution scope boundary.** Path-to-experiment resolution is scoped to the S3 artifact store's standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` layout (the standard RHOAI configuration, per the strategy's risk mitigation); limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented in E004 rather than solved.\n122\t\n123\t## Tiered Delivery\n124\t\n125\tNot applicable. All strategy dependencies are internal and existing (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy); there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1/Tier 2 split is needed. The parallelism that matters here is structural: the server/plugin chain (E001 → E003 → E004) and the operator chain (E002 → E005) proceed independently and converge in E006.\n126\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8bc00da466fea9","agent_id":"a4770eb0e41426a63","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-23c6bb583974","uuid":"d7d8da36-734d-4f5b-a9f4-789c845c6ab1","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:44:47.350Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-af7df17400c8ad89","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
