{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f051cf4363207d9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: MLflow server workspace-aware artifacts-only serving mode\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the workspace provider cannot be initialized without tracking\n16\t    stores, the server-side workspace-provider initialization approach changes to\n17\t    follow the plugin's refactored independent initialization path per the investigation's\n18\t    plan.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: -1\n25\t  external_dependency: 0\n26\t  human_process_gates: 0\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 4\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# MLflow server workspace-aware artifacts-only serving mode\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tMake `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` in the MLflow tracking server. When both flags are set with an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, the server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests, validates every artifact request against workspace membership (denying non-members with HTTP 403), and fails closed when the workspace store is unreachable. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer input).\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`; require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` in that combination — the server fails to start with a clear error if it is missing.\n45\t- Initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized, and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n46\t- For each artifact request: resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure — no new authorization mechanism and no new server flag.\n47\t- Fail-closed behavior: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, artifact requests are denied (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable), never served without validation.\n48\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without workspace flags retains current behavior; omitting either flag preserves current behavior; no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning.\n49\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (bundled here because it is emitted from the same denial code path the validation work builds; a different telemetry channel remains an open PM/Security question and would be follow-up scope).\n50\t- Measure the per-request workspace validation latency overhead and document the results; short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions (consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path) is deferred until the PM/Engineering latency threshold is set.\n51\t- Scope artifact path resolution to the S3 artifact store's standard `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/` structure (the standard RHOAI configuration); document limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n52\t\n53\t## Acceptance Criteria\n54\t\n55\t- The server starts with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and initializes the workspace provider while tracking and model registry stores remain uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n56\t- The server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but without `--workspace-store-uri` fails to start with a clear error.\n57\t- A user who is a member of workspace A requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A receives HTTP 200 with the correct artifact content.\n58\t- A user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B requesting an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B receives HTTP 403 with an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n59\t- With the workspace store unreachable, artifact requests are denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable — no artifact is served without validation.\n60\t- With `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, artifacts are served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved).\n61\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving produce audit log entries in the server logs.\n62\t- The per-request workspace validation latency overhead is measured and documented.\n63\t- No new server CLI flag is introduced, and no existing flag, API contract, or configuration key changes meaning.\n64\t\n65\t## HLR Traceability\n66\t\n67\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` — implemented here.\n68\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership with HTTP 403 denials — implemented here.\n69\t- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable — implemented here.\n70\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant and no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior — implemented here.\n71\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs — emitted from the same denial code path as HLR-2; not separately schedulable without a second pass over the same request-handling surface, so it is bundled at the higher epic priority.\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8bc00da466fea9","agent_id":"a4770eb0e41426a63","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-23c6bb583974","uuid":"ec469b0a-5ef9-4cbe-b374-9a1608dbd7e1","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:44:52.410Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f051cf4363207d9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
