pace-flag regression: deployments without `--enable-workspaces` retain current artifact serving behavior;\n44\t  - backwards compatibility: existing flag combinations, API contracts, and configuration keys are unchanged;\n45\t  - split-server routing: artifact traffic reaches the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the routing design selected by the routing investigation (gateway-level path routing or tracking-server delegation), and non-artifact traffic remains on the main tracking server.\n46\t- Wire the suite into CI.\n47\t- Document results against the strategy's acceptance criteria.\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- The automated suite covers every scenario listed in Scope and passes against the assembled split-server deployment.\n52\t- Each of the strategy's acceptance criteria — multi-tenant allow (HTTP 200) and deny (HTTP 403), current-behavior preservation without workspace flags, fail-closed denial (HTTP 503), operator creation of the distinct artifacts-only Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute, and the artifacts-only container command flags — is exercised by the suite with recorded results.\n53\t- Routing scenario coverage matches the routing approach that was implemented (gateway-level path routing or tracking-server delegation).\n54\t- The suite runs in CI without manual intervention.\n55\t\n56\t## HLR Traceability\n57\t\n58\t- Verifies HLR-2, HLR-3, HLR-4 (P0) end to end — the P0 behaviors are implemented and unit-verified within the server work; this epic provides assembled-topology verification.\n59\t- Verifies HLR-5, HLR-6, HLR-7 (P1) — the operator topology, artifact traffic routing, and plugin resolution in the artifacts-only context.\n60\t- No HLR is implemented directly by this epic; its priority (P1) reflects that its distinct scope is the assembled P1 topology plus holistic regression of the P0 behaviors.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9b8bc00da466fea9","agent_id":"a4770eb0e41426a63","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-