elected and its epics become issues.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nAll epics use canonical component `MLflow` (the canonical list has no separate MLflow Operator / mlflow-kubernetes-plugins entries — correctly handled and documented in Component Mapping Notes) and team MLflow Team, with boundaries drawn along repo/sub-system lines: E003 (plugin), E004 (server), E005 (operator), branch B split into server-side (BRANCH-B-E007) and operator-side (BRANCH-B-E008) halves per the component boundary rule. E005 at 2–3 sprints is one logical change (the split-server topology rendered through one Helm-in-operator chart extension); Health Warnings carry the estimate, the rationale, and a concrete conditional re-split trigger — watch item only per the size-classification rule. E003 vs E004 is complementary halves of one capability across separate components, not duplication (explicitly justified in the matrix). E004 vs E006 is unit-level verification vs assembled-topology integration testing — different levels, not a rebuilt surface, and the strategy explicitly allocates integration testing as its own work unit. No work-product type mixing; no enforcement-tool extraction; no bundling-recording gaps.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome determines verify-only vs. refactor for E003 and the initialization approach for E004 (both `gated_by` E001, `action: rewrite`, fallback populated); E002's outcome determines which branch epics exist (all three `gated_by` E002, `action: remove`, fallback populated). Every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. E006 lists no Investigation in its dependencies and correctly has no `gated_by` — no main-plan epic lists an Investigation without a gate. Signal sets are type-appropriate everywhere (Investigations carry `investigation_signals` with `ai_signals: null`; Implementations the reverse). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n\n##