E006 (P1) blocks nothing, and its P1 priority despite verifying P0 behaviors is explicitly justified in Health Warnings (the P0 behaviors are verified within E004's own server-level ACs per the strategy's ACs 1–4). Traceability matrix present and consistent with epic files.\n21\t\n22\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n23\t\n24\tNo cycles. Diagram ↔ frontmatter cross-check passes on every edge: E003 [E001], E004 [E001, E003], E005 [E002], E006 [E003, E004, E005], and each branch epic lists E002 (plus BRANCH-B-E008 lists sibling BRANCH-B-E007, permitted within-branch). Arrows follow the dependency→dependent convention. Every edge has a rule citation in the DAG Justification table. The E005 ∥ E003/E004 non-edge is exactly the rule-compliant shape: the operator renders the server's flag combination as a configuration reference, expressed as an AC gate on E005 (\"deployed against an MLflow server image supporting the workspace-aware artifacts-only flag combination\") rather than a `dependencies` edge, and validated in E006. No konflux/license/repo-onboarding epics exist (all components are in the platform), so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism is N/A. No docs-authoring epic exists and none is required — Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\" (Rule 11 does not trigger). Critical path E001 → E003 → E004 → E006 (length 4) is appropriate for an M-L strategy. Branch files each set both `branch` (matching filename) and `gated_by` E002, with `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` and populated fallbacks. Non-blocking observation (not an issue; the rule-compliant alternative is forbidden): E006's split-server routing scenario consumes the routing implementation delivered by whichever branch is selected; since main-plan epics may not depend on branch nodes and E002 is already transitively in E006's closure via E005, this scheduling coupling is inherently outside the main-plan DAG and should be re-checked when a branch is selected and its epics become issues.\n25\t\n26\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n27\t\n28\tAll epics use canonical component `MLflow` (the canonical list has no separate MLflow Operator / mlflow-kubernetes-plugins entries — correctly handled and documented in Component Mapping Notes) and team MLflow Team, with boundaries drawn along repo/sub-system lines: E003 (plugin), E004 (server), E005 (operator), branch B split into server-side (BRANCH-B-E007) and operator-side (BRANCH-B-E008) halves per the component boundary rule. E005 at 2–3 sprints is one logical change (the split-server topology rendered through one Helm-in-operator chart extension); Health Warnings carry the estimate, the rationale, and a concrete conditional re-split trigger — watch item only per the size-classification rule. E003 vs E004 is complementary halves of one capability across separate components, not duplication (explicitly justified in the matrix). E004 vs E006 is unit-level verification vs assembled-topology integration testing — different levels, not a rebuilt surface, and the strategy explicitly allocates integration testing as its own work unit. No work-product type mixing; no enforcement-tool extraction; no bundling-recording gaps.\n29\t\n30\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n31\t\n32\tBoth Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001's outcome determines verify-only vs. refactor for E003 and the initialization approach for E004 (both `gated_by` E001, `action: rewrite`, fallback populated); E002's outcome determines which branch epics exist (all three `gated_by` E002, `action: remove`, fallback populated). Every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies`. E006 lists no Investigation in its dependencies and correctly has no `gated_by` — no main-plan epic lists an Investigation without a gate. Signal sets are type-appropriate everywhere (Investigations carry `investigation_signals` with `ai_signals: null`; Implementations the reverse). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts.\n33\t\n34\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n35\t\n36\tSignal values verified against both rubrics and the strategy/architecture content. Investigation epics: E001 (`local_runnability: 1` — pip-installable plugin exercisable with the Kubernetes client stubbed; `cluster_hardware_dependence: 0` — the gating question is initialization-path coupling answerable from source) vs E002 (`local_runnability: 0`, `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` — peripheral HTTPRoute path-matching validation needs a live Gateway) is a well-differentiated, rubric-consistent pair. Implementation epics: `open_questions: -1` exactly on the gated epics (E003, E004, E005) and 0 on E006 and the branch epics whose gates resolve before start; `adapter_pattern` scored 0 where no adapter integration is the deliverable. `architecture_claims: 1` is well calibrated: the strategy's premises (the `--enable-workspaces`/`--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`/`kubernetes-auth` flag set, the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins v1.5.0 plugin, Dockerfile.konflux CMD, Helm-in-operator with server-side apply, conditional Gateway API CRD discovery) are corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/mlflow.md` and `mlflow-operator.md`, and each rationale names the corroborating file. Rationale files exist for all 9 epics and match the frontmatter values. BRANCH-B-E007's conservative `change_specificity: 0` / `pattern_precedent: 0` (delegation is a new capability with no in-codebase precedent) is honest scoring, not an error.\n37\t\n38\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n39\t\n40\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy — E004's ACs mirror strategy ACs 1–4 verbatim at server level (HTTP 200/403/503 semantics, fail-to-start on missing `--workspace-store-uri`, current-behavior preservation, no-new-flag) and E005's mirror ACs 5–6 (distinct Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute, exact container command including `--serve-artifacts`). Backwards-compatibility/feature-gating ACs are present for the behavior-composition change (activation only when both flags set). No konflux chain exists, so Rule 25 is N/A; the E005 image-support AC is the sanctioned AC-gate pattern, not a misfiled soft AC. No externally-controlled preconditions (lab turnaround, vendor timing, access grants) appear in any AC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — cross-epic coupling is expressed in prose (\"the routing design investigation\", \"the split-server topology chart work in the same repository\").\n41\t\n42\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n43\t\n44\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: every HLR, all six strategy acceptance criteria (server-level in E004, operator-level in E005, all exercised by E006's suite), and the strategy's explicitly allocated integration-testing work unit (E006). Strategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped: all four risks map into epic scope or Health Warnings (S3 path-resolution scope boundary in E004; plugin-coupling risk becomes E001/E003; split-topology misconfiguration risk becomes E005's CR validation AC; latency/caching deferral recorded with rework risk); all five \"needs validation\" assumptions have owners (three in E001, the Helm chart assumption in E005, single-tenant behavior in E004/E006 regression); all four open questions are dispositioned (two become the Investigations, latency and audit-channel noted in Health Warnings with follow-up scope). Out-of-scope boundaries are respected by every epic. Conditional branches cover both stated bounded outcomes of the routing question, with exactly one implemented per outcome. The docs-authoring check passes: Documentation Support is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\", so no docs-authoring epic is required, and the decision is explicitly documented.\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af2021c01c308886","agent_id":"a05eb870c6e614b5d","session_id":"63af744c-8e12-472e-a169-23c6bb583974","uuid":"94301134-fb5c-41a5-8e25-1c3b501b36f3","timestamp":"2026-10-26T15:49:57.552Z","tool_result_meta":[